A||||23||FieroKyle||Is K&N Air Filters that great???||||1||
Z||000000||FieroKyle||06-22-2001||01:55 AM||||Are K&Ns that great or are they way over rated. I hear they add lik 10-30 hp, but I don't really believe that. Any info would be appreciated, thanks.<P>Kyle - 85' GT||||reg||1
Z||000001||kinboyatuwo||06-22-2001||02:00 AM||||i have heard that they add between 2-5% at max<BR>so it all depends on the car<P><BR>have heard there is a huge diff with turbo cars<P>kin.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/kinboyatuwo/kinboyatuwo.jpg">    <BR>"Friends don't let friends drive four doors."<P>87 Duke, 235,000km||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||06-22-2001||02:09 AM||||The K&N's do not "add" horsepower. They actually just replenish horsepower lost thru restrictive air filters. The only thing I have noticed when I switched to a K&N was the car idled better and the throttle felt more sensative/responsive. As for actual horsepower gains it might have added 1-2 HP that was "lost" but no real gains. I did notice my gas mileage did get better by 1-2 miles per gallon. For the cost over time and the advantages with the idle and throttle response, I would say it is worth the swap. In fact all my cars now run a K&N. I clean mine every fall because here in Illinois we only have 2 seasons, Winter and Construction!<BR>The daily drivers get dirty with all the construction that goes on around here.||||reg||1
Z||000003||p4n1c||06-22-2001||08:11 AM||||Exactly what Oreif said.  I popped one into my car and I can feel a performance boost, but only because my air intake is very restrictive, it allowed more air to be sucked into the inlet.  <P>I've heard it does nothing for a Fiero except give you a reusable air filter, because of the design of the air intake.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4<BR>*Under Restoration*||||reg||1
Z||000004||1FST2M6||06-22-2001||08:18 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BlackGTdyno.jpg"> <P>the HP is ok but never having to replace it is worth the extra $. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/1fsthydrantsig.jpg">         <BR>www.engineered.net||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierobaby||06-22-2001||11:19 AM||||I must agree... each of my familys cars has one and the only car you can tell the difference with is the Dodge dayton that my brother owns we have a Ford escort A Jeep Cheeroke a Ford Aerostar a Chevy Mailbu and my Fiero I found no difference in anythign but the Daytona but it a turbo so thats most likely why<P>------------------<BR>1984 Sweet Candy Apple red Fiero sport coupe :-D<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/cfiero1.jpg"> ||||reg||10
Z||000006||Slammed Fiero||06-22-2001||11:32 AM||||Is the dodge daytona a turbo???<P>People get the misconception of power with K&N's. I have a cold air intake , I have had it for 5 years. I always find it funny when people claim huge improvements with their cold air setups and their K&N's. (my ultra custom air intake haha) I did it 5 years ago and there were lots of guys running around with them then.  <P>K&N advertises peak HP at a certian RPM. In a fiero a K&N with some sort of cold air setup generally yields about 1-3hp and takes away about 6-9ft lbs of torque.<P>Now , 1FST2M6 got some improvement at the stip , but i think his setup is the exception.<P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">      <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals. &lt;b&gt; New White Face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS&lt;/b&gt;Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierobaby||06-22-2001||11:40 AM||||yes the Dayton is a turbo :-)||||reg||1
Z||000008||p4n1c||06-22-2001||11:48 AM||||Is it a Shelby-Z?  My uncle had one.  Man that thing flew.  He gave my grandparents a 10 minute headstart(my grandfather usually does 100-110kph on the highway) and he passed and beat them to the restaurant.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>He had done a few modifications to the engine.  Not really sure what, but some car tried to race us one time and he nailed it off the line, then the turbo spooled up and we were gone.<P>2.2l 4-cyl 174hp 200ft/lb torque stock for the Shelby-Z.<P>------------------<BR>-=-=-=[ p4n1c ]=-=-=-<BR>1988 Olds Cutlass Supreme International<BR>2.8L MPFI V6/TH440-T4<BR>*Under Restoration*||||reg||1
Z||000009||HarryG||06-22-2001||12:05 PM||||I recall seeing a magazine article by David Vizard where he tested air cleaners on his flow bench.  There was a wide variation in CFM rates among brands.  Think K & N was near the top.  (Check your old copies of Popular Hot Rodding or Car Craft) This article cured me of buying "blue light special" air cleaners! ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Slammed Fiero||06-22-2001||12:14 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierobaby:<BR><B>yes the Dayton is a turbo :-)</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Turbo engines work on a different pricipal than an "atmospheric" engines. Thats why I had a feeling it was a turbo ..more air , = more power regaurdless.<P>Regular N/A (naturally aspirated) motors require backpressure , Turbo's work best with no back pressure at all. (no restriction)<P>I used to drive a 66 Corvair Corsa Turbo to school. 180Hp , I never even ran a muffler , Just had a 3" dump from the turbo to the ground. The car was setup with a side draft webber. No restriction meant the turbo came on like hard and was eager to spool. Only adverse effect (in some peoples minds) was the flame that would come out when I shifted... <P>That was a fast car..<P>------------------<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1607505&a=12259132&p=48230089&Sequence=0&res=high">       <BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals. &lt;b&gt; New White Face Gauges , 75Hp Shot of NOS&lt;/b&gt;Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.<p>[This message has been edited by Slammed Fiero (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||06-22-2001||02:23 PM||||I have a turbo and I dont really know about any difference. I got it cause water dont destroy it all the time, and I can use it for years. come to think of it, i ought to clean and reoil it, its been 2 years...||||reg||1
Z||000012||terryk||06-22-2001||04:17 PM||||And when pressed, K&N will admit that fine dust particles will strip the oil from the filter making it filter even less. But hey, it flows more!<P>When you compare apples to apples, K&N's do flow more because they filter less.<P>GM hates them with a passion. Wonder why. <P>I have one on my car but I clean and oil it every month.<P>Maybe someone can answer this: Where is the K&N air filter on that top fueler they show in the commerical?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Dave Gunsul||06-22-2001||08:23 PM||||LOL! You said a mouth full about the construction around here Oreif! <BR>Will they ever actually FIX anything or are<BR>they just going to keep ripping it all up??<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||10
Z||000014||Brucepts||06-22-2001||10:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by p4n1c:<BR><B>Exactly what Oreif said.  I popped one into my car and I can feel a performance boost, but only because my air intake is very restrictive, it allowed more air to be sucked into the inlet.  <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>To clarify a point made here . . . air does not get sucked into the engine it mearly flows from a high pressure area to a low pressure area created by the piston displacement.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||1
