Z||000018||DaRkLoRD||06-23-2001||12:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Brucepts:<BR><B> To clarify a point made here . . . air does not get sucked into the engine it mearly flows from a high pressure area to a low pressure area created by the piston displacement.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Same goes for every kind of flow, be it water, air, or electrons.. Ladies and Gentlemen, THERE IS NO SUCK.. heh<P>(I'm tired. can anyone tell??)<P>------------------<BR>    <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>    <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||10
Z||000019||FieroKyle||06-23-2001||01:08 AM||||Thanks for the info!<BR><P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/ViewPhoto?u=1736156&a=13354853&p=50784879&f=0[/IMG]" TARGET=_blank>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/ViewPhoto?u=1736156&a=13354853&p=50784879&f=0[/IMG]</A>   <BR>My Fast 'n Black Car<P>Kyle - 85' GT||||reg||1
Z||000020||FieroKyle||06-23-2001||01:09 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/ViewPhoto?u=1736156&a=13354853&p=50784879&f=0"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||Formula88||06-23-2001||11:28 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaRkLoRD:<BR><B> Same goes for every kind of flow, be it water, air, or electrons.. Ladies and Gentlemen, THERE IS NO SUCK.. heh<P>(I'm tired. can anyone tell??)<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Define "suck"-ing air.  Wouldn't that be when you have a device that pulls air from one place to another, i.e. creates a low pressure area so the ambient pressure is higher and forces the flow, as opposed to creating a high pressure to push air from one place to another, which I would call "blow"-ing.<P>"Sucking air" is an entirely correct statement to describe what a normally aspirated internal combustion engine does to gets it's intake air.<P>As for the filter, yeah, it might help you free up a couple of horsepower lost to the factory air cleaner.  The biggest advantage is never having to replace them and the fact that they are more durable.||||reg||6
Z||000022||Brucepts||06-24-2001||06:19 PM||||Technically you have 14.7psi of pressure pushing the air into a low pressure area on a normally asperated engine so it is not "sucking" air at all. It is creating a low pressure area which is being filled via the higher pressure. Pickup a good fluid dynamics book and you can see it explained in more techincal terms. I recommend the book Scientific Design of Exhaust & Intake Systems; Smith & Morrison as excellent reference material  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Advanced Dealer in the Great Material Continuum . . .<BR>Non Fiero Website: <A HREF="http://tractorsport.com" TARGET=_blank>http://tractorsport.com</A> <BR>You can't race a dyno . . .||||reg||1
Z||000023||Alex4mula||06-25-2001||08:38 AM||||Too many engineers here... Just buy the K&N. You won't be disappointed.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||6
